{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Convolutional Stride.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Convolutional Stride.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"convolutional stride convolutional stride","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Convolutional Stride.md","fileName":"Convolutional Stride.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Convolutional Stride","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 02:14\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Convolutional Visual Architecture]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Convolutional Visual Architecture","CNN Receptive Field","Hyperparameter","Convolutional Feature Map","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Convolutional Stride.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Convolutional Stride.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"convolutional stride convolutional stride","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Convolutional Stride.md","fileName":"Convolutional Stride.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Convolutional Stride","heading":"Convolutional Stride","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Stride length is the distance a convolutional detector moves between successive locations on an input. In the book's image example, a stride of one moves each [[CNN Receptive Field|receptive field]] one position from its neighbor, so the fields overlap substantially. Increasing stride reduces that overlap.\n\nStride is a [[Hyperparameter]] chosen before training rather than a kernel weight learned from data. It affects which local regions are inspected and how densely their responses appear in the [[Convolutional Feature Map]]. A larger step can make the map coarser, so the stride should match the scale of the patterns the architecture needs to preserve.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Convolutional Visual Architecture","CNN Receptive Field","Hyperparameter","C